 ## 3. Contracts
 
@@ -72,6 +76,37 @@
 delete statement, under the runtime fence. Retained business runs, reservations
 and termination evidence keep their existing lifetime.
 
+For host version 2, a previously persisted `phaseProof` receives proof-ACK
+priority before telemetry. `phaseAcknowledged` may still retain checkpoint,
+outcome or seal recovery, but must never Claim, Permit, refresh handoff input,
+start a Provider, or submit the acknowledged proof again. Original stop and
+launch-termination observations remain independent of recording success.
+
+Checkpoint, outcome and seal reporting share at most ten seconds, shortened to
+half the caller's remaining deadline in a bounded recovery round. Checkpoint
+replay additionally has a two-second total limit, with one-second requests
+derived from that context. Per-chunk timeouts alone can consume the entire
+shared round. A failed report retains its original pending payload; it does not
+prevent the original proof attempt with the remaining caller context.
+
+An uncertain seal is replayed with identical identity, attempt, nonce, sequence,
+reason and bounds. After its ACK, a newly learned checkpoint watermark may
+require a same-reason sequence+1. Keep that advance pending in the ledger and
+stage it only once every chunk has resolved, so successive bounded passes do
+not exhaust the 32-seal limit. Never mutate an uncertain seal to reflect a newer
+ACK. The service derives retained content from committed events, independently
+of the sender's acknowledged count.
+
+`Client.RecordingSeal` requires both retention-bound fields to be present;
+`retained_through` is a bounded integer and `observed_through` is a bounded
+integer or explicit null. Missing fields, null retained counts, negative/out-of-
+range counts and observed counts below retained counts are `ErrProtocol` and
+cannot clear pending recovery. Reuse the shared seal-bound validator. A
+`completed` sender observation requires a known observed bound, but does not
+prove that every observed message was retained; a known missing tail remains a
+valid, incomplete retention fact. Later facts may increase returned bounds on
+an identical old-seal ACK.
+
